## Langfuse redesigns the trace view so large agent traces are easier to read

Source: Langfuse — https://langfuse.com/changelog/2026-11-20-redesigned-trace-view
Published: 2026-11-20
Channel: news

Langfuse is an open-source platform that records and evaluates what LLM applications and agents do, including traces of each step in a run. A trace is the record of one run and the observations inside it. The company says its trace view now looks different than it did a few months ago, but the redesign arrived in many small steps, so people may not have noticed each change.

Large agent traces can be hard to read: the overall shape of a long run gets lost, and the input, output, and metadata of the step you are debugging sit among a lot of surrounding clutter. Layouts can also change when you move from the full trace page to a side peek or to a smaller screen.

The new view addresses that in three ways. First, the Timeline and graph view show the shape of a large agent trace at a glance, so you can zoom into the step that matters. Then, the view focuses on the input, output, and metadata of the observation you are debugging, with less clutter around them. Finally, the same layout is used on the full trace page, in the side peek, and on smaller screens, so you do not have to relearn the page when you change context.

Why it matters: If you debug long agent traces in Langfuse, open a recent large trace in the Timeline or graph view and confirm you can find the failing step, then check that the side peek still shows the same input, output, and metadata layout.

