## CloudWatch adds built-in dashboards for inference servers on EKS

Source: Amazon Web Services — https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/11/cloudwatch-eks-kubernetes-tools/
Published: 2026-11-20
Channel: news

Amazon CloudWatch is AWS's monitoring service. AWS says EKS Container Insights, its monitoring for Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service clusters, now includes built-in dashboards for open source tools on those clusters, including the inference servers vLLM, KServe, and SGLang, plus Karpenter, KEDA, Ray, and Knative Serving, without running Prometheus or Grafana yourself.

Monitoring starts automatically when you enable OpenTelemetry Container Insights. For AI inference workloads, you can see queued requests, time to first token, and key-value cache usage for each model server. For autoscaling, you can see which scalers are failing and how long the scale loop takes. CloudWatch tags every metric with its pod, workload, and node, so you can trace a slow model server to the node it runs on.

Why it matters: If you already run inference servers on EKS with OpenTelemetry Container Insights, open the new dashboards and confirm that queue depth, time to first token, and cache usage appear for each model server, tagged to the correct node.

![CloudWatch adds built-in dashboards for inference servers on EKS](/edition-images/2026-11-21/cloudwatch-eks.webp)

## Google's Agent Anomaly Detection can watch agents that run outside Google Cloud

Source: Google Developers Blog — https://developers.googleblog.com/agent-anomaly-detection-safeguarding-agents-across-other-clouds/
Published: 2026-11-20
Channel: news

Agent Anomaly Detection is a Google Cloud preview service that reviews an agent's traces and logs to flag behavior that drifts outside intended boundaries. Google says it is in private preview, including monitoring for agents that run outside Google Cloud. Telemetry, including the prompts and responses the service reads, goes to your Google Cloud project, while the agent's model and tool calls still run where the agent runs.

To enroll an agent elsewhere, you send OpenTelemetry traces and logs to Google Cloud, describe where the agent runs, and wait until it appears in the Monitored agents list, which the post says happens within thirty minutes. After you enable it, the service starts analyzing sessions. In a test on AWS Lambda, a shopper who checked a few items was not flagged, while a scraper paging through a catalog was flagged for cascading failures and resource exhaustion,. In a Microsoft Foundry test, sessions that pushed a customer-service agent into unverified refunds were flagged, including an order note that contained override instructions.

The post says that today this path supports agents built with Google's Agent Development Kit for Python, and that findings for agents outside Google Cloud appear only in the Agent Anomaly Detection console. To get started, Google asks teams to request access to the private preview.

Why it matters: If you already run ADK agents outside Google Cloud and can send their traces there, request preview access and confirm that findings appear in the Agent Anomaly Detection console, remembering that this path is a preview and currently limited to ADK for Python.

