## Dynatrace puts traces and cost signals into Gemini Enterprise workflows

Source: Dynatrace — https://www.dynatrace.com/news/blog/dynatrace-and-google-cloud-deepen-partnership-to-bring-ai-observability-to-gemini-enterprise/
Published: 2026-11-20
Channel: news

Dynatrace is an observability platform that records traces, metrics, and logs across applications and infrastructure. A company blog says operating applications, agents, and workflows that use Google's Gemini models in production is harder than running a pilot: teams need to see how those systems behave, why they fail, what they cost, and how they affect customer experiences.

The expanded collaboration with Google Cloud, as Dynatrace describes it, has three parts. First, Dynatrace for Gemini Enterprise brings real-time observability data, Grail data, causal insights, and automation into Gemini Enterprise agent workflows, so teams can investigate issues in natural language without switching tools. The post says, for example, that an engineer can investigate a latency increase on an AI-enabled customer transaction and identify the models, agents, services, or infrastructure involved. Then, expanded observability for Gemini-enabled environments is meant to trace AI activity, monitor latency and token consumption, manage costs, and connect technical performance to business outcomes. Finally, the integrations are available through Google Cloud Marketplace.

The post also says agents in those workflows can access distributed traces, topology, business events, and root-cause insights, and that Dynatrace data is available in the Google Antigravity command-line tool. Arize, a Dynatrace company, is described as the AI-native tracing and evaluation layer, with Dynatrace covering applications, services, and infrastructure.

Why it matters: If you run Gemini-enabled agents and already use Dynatrace or Google Cloud procurement, review the Marketplace listing and confirm whether traces, token use, and latency for those agents appear in the same investigation workflow the post describes; treat the operational claims as vendor-stated.

