## Datadog lets AI agents draft and check telemetry pipelines, with limits on what they can change

Source: Datadog — https://www.datadoghq.com/blog/observability-pipelines-mcp-server-ai-agents/
Published: 2026-11-20
Channel: news

Datadog Observability Pipelines is a product that collects, transforms, and routes logs and metrics in your own environment before they reach their destinations. Datadog says the work of onboarding a source, finding where events drop, validating a change, and measuring volume reduction is repetitive, and that an AI agent can help if it can see pipeline configurations and metrics.

The company says Observability Pipelines tools are now generally available in the Datadog MCP Server, a connector that lets agents in clients such as Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, and VS Code call Datadog tools. In the create-from-a-prompt flow, the agent first discovers available sources, processors, and destinations and their required fields, then validates the draft without creating or deploying anything, and finally creates a new pipeline that does not process data until you install the Observability Pipelines Worker for it. The same tools can describe a pipeline and query worker metrics, including events received and sent, intentional and unintentional discards, destination errors, and buffer size, to answer whether events are being dropped.

The toolset cannot update or delete existing pipelines. Creating a pipeline requires the Observability Pipelines Deploy permission, and the user who connects the agent also needs the matching MCP read or write permission. Datadog says your team still decides when a new pipeline starts processing data.

Why it matters: If you already use Observability Pipelines, enable the toolset in a client under a read-only role first, ask the agent to describe a pipeline and its drop metrics, and only grant deploy permission after you have reviewed how new pipelines are created without changing existing ones.

